title: add_crd fails for a CRD with a property named like a Moo keyword or imported
  type (after, before, with, extends, has, meta, import, Str, ...)
updated: 2026-10-18T06:47:07Z
---

`IO::K8s::Resource::_setup_class` (lib/IO/K8s/Resource.pm:241-249) imports Moo and the type names into every class it sets up - shipped, inline-struct and AutoGen alike - and only `HashRef`/`Opaque` are swept again (line 234-239). So every class still `can` `has`, `with`, `extends`, `before`, `after`, `around`, `k8s`, `Str`, `Int`, `Bool`, `Num`, `IntOrStr`, `Quantity`, `Time`, next to Moo's own `new`, `meta`, `does`, `import`, `BUILD`:

```
perl -Ilib -MIO::K8s -e 'require IO::K8s::Api::Core::V1::PodSpec; print join(",", grep { IO::K8s::Api::Core::V1::PodSpec->can($_) } qw(has with extends before after around k8s Str Int)), "\n"'
```

The declaration preflight (k144, lib/IO/K8s/Resource.pm:562-567) then refuses any schema property of one of these names with 'collides with the method ...', and because a generation run fails as a whole, the entire CRD is unusable. That is not exotic: Kong's `KongPlugin` has `ordering.after` / `ordering.before`. A CRD of that shape:

```
add_crd: k8s: field 'after' of ...::KongPlugin::Ordering collides with the method 'after' of ...::KongPlugin::Ordering, which is not an attribute
```

The AutoGen POD only documents the top-level case (role methods such as `label`, `save`) and says of nested classes 'these names are ordinary fields there' (lib/IO/K8s/AutoGen.pm:2146-2161), which does not hold for the names above. Two JSON keys that sanitize to one accessor (`a-b` and `a_b`) end the same way.

Done when: the compile-time sugar (Moo keywords, `k8s`, the type names) is removed from each class once its declarations are done, so those names are free as fields; a property that still collides with a real method (`new`, `meta`, `does`, `can`, `isa`, `import`, `TO_JSON`, ...) gets a renamed accessor with the JSON key kept via the existing json_key/init_arg mapping instead of failing the CRD; t/27_no_import_leak.t covers the keyword names and a test loads a CRD with `after`/`before`/`with` properties.

